WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:03.397
A frozen model, and a memory bolted on beside it.

00:00:03.777 --> 00:00:07.099
Past turns go in on the right as a few final-layer states,

00:00:07.099 --> 00:00:10.421
and come back out as keys and values for every attention layer.

00:00:13.127 --> 00:00:13.904
I'm Engram.

00:00:13.904 --> 00:00:18.564
Let's follow one conversation into MemOnce's memory and one question back out.

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:24.040
Each turn runs through the model once.

00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:27.716
Only the final-layer states of its last 64 tokens are kept.

00:00:27.716 --> 00:00:29.388
The rest is thrown away.

00:00:29.808 --> 00:00:33.465
The newest 16 blocks stay on the GPU as a short window,

00:00:33.465 --> 00:00:34.683
read on every question.

00:00:35.103 --> 00:00:36.790
A question gets one key.

00:00:36.790 --> 00:00:40.502
One cosine pass over the older blocks picks the top 64.

00:00:40.502 --> 00:00:43.876
That choice is made once, before any token is generated.

00:00:44.296 --> 00:00:48.107
Each attention layer decodes the same blocks into its own keys and values.

00:00:48.107 --> 00:00:51.625
A block that missed the cut is invisible to all of them.

00:00:53.233 --> 00:00:54.357
Here is the formula.

00:00:55.233 --> 00:00:58.420
Qwen three point five, four B, has eight attention layers.

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:00.985
Each keeps a key and a value per token.

00:01:02.276 --> 00:01:03.990
Thirty-two kibibytes a token.

00:01:03.990 --> 00:01:06.562
A million tokens is thirty-two gibibytes.

00:01:08.233 --> 00:01:12.480
The headline divides thirty-two gigabytes of full cache by twenty-five megabytes of

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:14.249
states, before they are decoded.

00:01:14.669 --> 00:01:18.623
Decoded for eight layers, those states are a hundred and sixty mebibytes.

00:01:18.623 --> 00:01:20.270
Still flat at any length.

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:24.224
And the whole stored pool is fifty-one times smaller than full KV.

00:01:26.108 --> 00:01:29.084
Reading everything is quadratic in the attention layers.

00:01:29.084 --> 00:01:32.433
About two hundred eighty-seven seconds at a million tokens.

00:01:32.853 --> 00:01:35.337
MemOnce writes in short segments: forty-one seconds.

00:01:35.337 --> 00:01:37.465
That is the six point nine.

00:01:37.885 --> 00:01:39.672
But look at the short end.

00:01:39.672 --> 00:01:41.757
At thirty-two thousand tokens MemOnce is slower.

00:01:43.508 --> 00:01:46.205
I ran the released checkpoint on a 3090.

00:01:46.205 --> 00:01:50.251
At a hundred and thirty-two thousand tokens, writing took about half the

00:01:50.251 --> 00:01:54.297
time of a full prefill, and the decoded cache measured exactly a

00:01:54.297 --> 00:01:55.645
hundred and sixty mebibytes.

00:01:56.867 --> 00:01:57.987
The memory benchmarks.

00:01:57.987 --> 00:02:02.841
I recomputed every MemOnce column from the released result files, and they match.

00:02:03.221 --> 00:02:06.161
It wins most where reading everything drowns.

00:02:06.161 --> 00:02:08.681
On LoCoMo, full context still wins.

00:02:10.025 --> 00:02:14.162
The price: a turn survives only as its tail, and retrieval happens once.

00:02:14.162 --> 00:02:17.981
In my needle test the right block was retrieved, and the answer

00:02:17.981 --> 00:02:19.890
was still wrong once in three.

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:21.532
Only each turn's tail.

00:02:23.019 --> 00:02:24.801
One retrieval, before answering.

00:02:25.728 --> 00:02:26.966
Keys without rotation.

00:02:28.214 --> 00:02:29.829
Linear layers hold no past.

00:02:32.183 --> 00:02:36.274
Store six hundred forty bytes a token, attend over a fixed hundred

00:02:36.274 --> 00:02:39.342
and sixty mebibytes, and bet everything on one retrieval.

00:02:40.608 --> 00:02:43.924
MemOnce: write once, retrieve once, decode for every layer.

00:02:45.208 --> 00:02:47.309
Every source is in the full article.

00:02:47.309 --> 00:02:47.909
I'm Engram.

00:02:47.909 --> 00:02:48.209
Bye!
